1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,278
On the day, the book is down 678 dollars.

7
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:32,609
APA Corporation (APA) leads, +0.6 percent.

8
00:00:32,609 --> 00:00:38,074
State Street SPDR S&P Biotech ETF (XBI) lags at -4.2.

9
00:00:38,074 --> 00:00:43,642
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:43,642 --> 00:00:47,168
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:55,000
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:04,048
12 positions, led by APA Corporation, ticker APA, Chord Energy, ticker CHRD, Credit Acceptance, ticker CACC.

13
00:01:04,048 --> 00:01:15,442
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:20,290
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:22,731
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:22,731 --> 00:01:33,456
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:33,456 --> 00:01:37,467
Inside the book today: 7 positions up, 5 down.

18
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:39,909
Best, BRKR at +1.73 percent.

19
00:01:39,909 --> 00:01:41,653
Worst, BGC at -1.53.

20
00:01:41,653 --> 00:01:46,710
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:53,947
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -6.45% per trade over 260 graded positions.

22
00:01:53,947 --> 00:02:01,097
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:02:01,097 --> 00:02:06,329
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:06,329 --> 00:02:18,885
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 225 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:18,844 --> 00:02:21,089
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:21,089 --> 00:02:30,348
Day 41 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:30,348 --> 00:02:35,212
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 101,942.

28
00:02:35,212 --> 00:02:42,133
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 1.6 thousand back at 100,317.

29
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:49,335
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.4 thousand back at 98,546.

30
00:02:49,335 --> 00:02:51,954
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:51,954 --> 00:02:56,257
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:56,257 --> 00:03:01,120
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:02,991
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:02,991 --> 00:03:05,320
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

35
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:11,782
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

36
00:03:11,782 --> 00:03:15,238
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

37
00:03:15,238 --> 00:03:24,255
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

38
00:03:24,214 --> 00:03:31,549
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:31,549 --> 00:03:33,791
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:33,791 --> 00:03:36,779
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
